Wood, though one of the oldest structural materials, is still one of the most common and useful ones. It is heavily utilized by plants, being necessary for the height to which trees are able to grow to. Furthermore, it is important for humans as well, where wood is central to modern engineering and construction. It is in everything from family homes to multi-story buildings and long-span bridges, second only to concrete in its usage as a material. Wood's widespread use can be traced economically to its abundance, renewability, and low hazard risk when used. More importantly, the combination of a high strength-to-weight ratio, ease of machining, and low density makes wood a practical choice for beams, joists, frames, and other common designs. These unique properties also make wood a rich subject for scientific study.

In contrast to many other building materials like steel or concrete, wood is renewable and stores carbon as part of its time in a living tree. This gives wood its distinctive properties that can vary widely depending on the microstructure of the specific type and even the specific piece of wood being investigated. At the same time, this also makes wood an essential material for continuing and expanding upon sustainable, low-carbon construction. However, wood faces difficulties due to anisotropy, moisture sensitivity, and the ability to rot (biological degradation), thus spurring research into treatments and engineered wood products that can enhance its structural performance and durability. 

This project examines those structurally important properties of wood with regards to strength and looks at one method of enhancing said properties, aiming to expand where and how wood can be safely and efficiently used.

== Wood as a Structural and Engineering Material
It is important to keep in mind when investigating the properties of wood that it exists to serve three main functions: to conduct water up the plant from the roots to the leaves, to provide mechanical support and a foundation for the body of the plant, and to store biochemicals for potential use later. Every property of wood can be derived from the fact that wood must meet the demands of the tree that it evolved out of @wiedenhoeft_structure.

Wood possesses some key advantages for its use in engineering and construction: its high strength-to-weight ratio, relatively low density, ease for use in machining and joining together, high abundance, and its renewable nature. Beyond just the mechanical properties, wood is an excellent thermal and acoustic insulator. These qualities lead to wood being most widely used in buildings, bridges, flooring, furniture, utility poles, and other similar structures.

As mentioned before, wood shows remarkable strength when compared to its lower weight. This strength is most impressive in flexing and compression, and even more so along the grain. This allows wood to be used in carrying significant loads without excessive weight from the building material itself. This property of wood originates from it being mostly comprised of air, leading to a significantly lower density, while the cellulose fibers packed within it are the actual source of its strength @markwardt_wood.

When compared with metals, wood is much softer and thus easier to work with, simplifying cutting, drilling, planing, shaping, and reworking the material in any other way. Often, only simple tools with lower power requirements are necessary for woodworking. That softer surface also makes wood a better fit for nails, screws, and adhesives. With these additives, wood pieces are easily joined together through the use of common construction techniques @sekin_2008_wood.

Even with wood's unique properties, it is still not an excellent building material, especially for larger, longer-lasting structures that cannot afford to rot or be damaged by the elements in other ways. Nevertheless, to complement wood's material properties is its overwhelming abundance that no other material on earth can match. Trees cover large swaths of the world, with many of these species suitable for structural lumber and even able to be cultivated in plantations. Metals may require mining and concrete may require mixing, but trees can be seen almost everywhere @properties.

Wood's abundance stems directly from another one of its desirable properties: its renewability and sustainability of usage. Wood can be easily replanted and regrown, so via sustainable management of forests, the resource can be renewed over time @navi_2004_combined. During their growth, trees absorb and utilize carbon dioxide from the atmosphere, releasing the oxygen as a byproduct and storing the carbon in the wood, where it will stay for decades. In this way, wood from trees can be used to hold atmospheric carbon, thus helping prevent some of the nastier consequences of global warming @cristvo_2013_machining.

== Mechanical Limitations of Natural Wood
For all of its excellent properties, that is not to say wood has no disadvantages of its own when compared to other building materials. Most unique among its undesirable properties are wood's anisotropy, or having varying strength depending on direction of load. Wood is composed of compacted cellulose fibers that are bound together by hemicellulose and lignin molecules. The cellulose fibers are the strongest component, however, and thus wood is much stronger and stiffer in the grain direction than perpendicular to it. As a result, the tensile and compressive strength perpendicular to the grain is relatively weak, and wood appears to be brittle along this direction as well. Due to this anisotropy, wood is limited on how it can be oriented, how connections can be made, and how weight is upheld. Wood is therefore prone to splitting, crushing, and failing at points where loads press across the grain, such as in supports or bolt groups.

Wood also faces the problem of having a high amount of natural variability and higher likelihood of defect than many other materials. Wood is a biological material and grows non-uniformly. However, for construction, it is preferred to have standardized, uniform materials that are broadly applicable @markwardt_wood.

There is also considerable structural differences between various types of woods, the most notable of which being the separation of hardwoods and softwoods. Hardwood trees shed their leaves, which are large and broad, in autumn. Fruits or nuts enclose their seeds in protective casings. Softwoods are usually evergreen with needle-like leaves and grow faster, making them more desirable for farming. In general, hardwoods are denser, tougher, higher density, have more complex grain patterns, cost more, and grow slower, while softwoods are the opposite. Thus, for the purposes of load-bearing and construction, hardwoods tend to be much more desirable than softwoods @kou_2023_hardwood. There are some softwoods whose strength can rival or even exceed some hardwoods. However, these are the exception, not the rule.

Between wood and other building materials, wood's biological nature presents many problems for construction. Defects can form much more easily in wood, in the form of knots, grain deviation, checks, splits, and more can form, significantly reducing strength when compared with "ideal" specimens. As wood specimens get larger, by the rules of probability, the chance of a defect only increases. This gives wood a limitation important to consider in what its ideal strength would be when compared to the maximum attainable results when adjusted for real world difficulties. For these reasons, there exists a grading scale for the quality of the lumber obtained, which is important to consider for what application the wood is being used for. In contrast, by the nature of their chemistry, metal pieces are much more uniform and are not anisotropic either, making it far simpler to design ways to use those materials @green_1999_mechanical. Thus, when calculating load-bearing amounts for wood pieces within a design, conservative strength assumptions must be used so as to not push the wood beyond its limits.

But wood is also sensitive to moisture, much more so than just the simple rusting exhibited by some metals. Wood will swell and shrink in response to moisture changes, leading to all sorts of internal stress and defects, including warping and cupping. At high moisture contents and in prolonged wet conditions especially, wood will demonstrate reduced strength and stiffness. Any large-scale usage of wood for a construction project thus also requires methods to address moisture behavior @sekin_2008_wood.

Beyond moisture, wood's biological "roots," so to speak, also makes biological attack a potential issue. In moist, poorly ventilated conditions, fungi and mold will eat away at wood fibers, destroying it and annihilating whatever strength it had @kirker_2018_wood. In certain climates, insects like termites or beetles feast upon wood, adding another difficulty to consider in its usage @yalcin_2020_damage. Environmental factors are also important. UV rays emitted from the sun and weathering from environmental conditions will contribute to a loss of surface strength, breaking the wood apart layer by layer. Most notably, wood is susceptible to fire, which other building materials often do not have a problem with @mensah_2023_characterisation.

All of these factors culminate in the last problem with wood: in terms of simply strength, natural wood alone cannot rival any of the major modern building materials. Previous research @franciscoarriagamartitegui_2023_mechanical has shown that wood's far lower density, while leading to a very high strength-to-weight ratio, ends up adversely impacting absolute mechanical properties. Wood shows an absolute tensile strength of #qtyrange(70, 165, "MPa") along the grain, with hardwoods on the upper end and softwoods at the lower end. In contrast, structural steel, ubiquitous as a modern building material, already has a tensile strength of #qtyrange(250, 550, "MPa") and go much higher through the use of advanced alloying. Since steel is isotropic and fails by yielding, its compressive strength, flexural strength, and shear strength are also incredibly high values. Wood fails by crushing under compressive load, leading to a very low compressive strength and shear strength, while its flexural strength is much higher because of the nature of cellulose fibers. Nevertheless, all of these metrics point to wood having favorable specific properties (strength relative to density) but poor absolute properties.

== Previous Approaches to Strength Enhancement
Because of these poor absolute strength properties, much research has been done into ways to potentially augment these values through specialized treatment of wood samples. These treatments can be broadly divided along the lines of mechanical treatments, chemical treatments, and hybrid treatments.

The simplest among the mechanical treatments is restructuring wood on the macro-scale to create stronger composites. This practice is known more broadly as engineering wood products, working by mechanically rearranging wood. Examples of this include plywood, glue-laminated timber (glulam), and cross-laminated timber (CLT). These methods help average out natural defects, allow for the creation of larger wood pieces than can normally be cut from a tree, and stabilize strength values across directions. By making more efficient use of wood fibers, engineered wood can even exceed the performance of traditional lumber products within many structural niches. However, these methods more so rearrange and bind wood than deal with the issue of natural wood itself, leading to a density and strength still constrained by the boundaries imposed by natural wood. They are still moisture sensitive by nature @jin_2025_life. Furthermore, many manufacturing processes use synthetic adhesives that remove any notion of sustainability and recyclability of these products, while also being more expensive and less visually appealing for designers and architects.

While engineered wood attempts to address some of the macro-scale blemishes in natural wood, two main methods have been developed in previous studies to improve the mechanical properties and hardness of wood by operating on the micro-structure of wood itself. These methods are mechanical compression and chemical impregnation. Traditional densification by mechanical compression is based on the concept of compressing wood samples to lower their thickness, thereby increasing their density and thus their strength and hardness. Enormous pressure is applied perpendicular to the grain, which under normal circumstances leads to the wood samples expanding radially from the point of compression instead of actually densifying @cabral_2022_densification. Thus, a constraining apparatus of some sort is used to restrict the ability of the wood to expand in other directions. Water and moisture is squeezed out; the previously mentioned air pockets within the wood are forced to collapse under this immense pressure, increasing the density of the wood. As the mechanical strength of wood is highly correlated to its density @niklas_2010_worldwide,  this process also leads to the strengthening of the wood.

However, when compressed in this way, wood does not deform in a plastic manner, and will elastically rebound over time due to an effect known as _springback_ @cabral_2024_a. While the wood would theoretically hold its compressed shape, exposure to moisture and heat can cause excessive swelling and replace all of the water that had been squeezed out during the compressive process. The wood is then essentially restored to the same state it was originally and will rebound back to its original shape @morsing_1998_densification. This removes any potential benefit from this compressive process, and is thus most undesirable.

Previous studies have shown that one of the cheapest and most effective ways to prevent the springback effect from restoring a wood sample to its original shape is by combining the mechanical compression with heating, a process which is known as _hot pressing_ @navi_2004_combined. The theory behind why this method is effective originates in the nature of springback itself. The phenomenon is a result of primary bonds between layers of cellulose fibers in the wood being retained despite the compression, as a product of lignin macromolecules bundling cellulose fibers together. The lignin is compressed along with the rest of the wood in the sample, but will expand and retain its original shape given time. To prevent this, heating to high temperatures can be used to break those primary bonds between layers, reducing springback and possibly even augmenting the stress of the compressed wood sample by reducing internal stresses @belleville_2025_effect.

On the other end of potential treatments for these wood samples to improve their strength lies the chemical methods. The basis of these methods are to fill those aforementioned air pockets with a chemical substance to improve dimensional stability, increased hardness, better decay resistance, and sometimes even increase the strength or stiffness of the material.

A vast number of substances have been considered for usage in chemical impregnation. Acetylation, or impregnation by acetic anhydride, leads to the esterification of available hydroxyl groups in the cell wall, forming acetic acid as a by-product to be subsequently removed during processing @dmitriiefremov_2023_chemical. Furfurylation uses the liquid of furfuryl alcohol obtained from agricultural waste (ie. corn cobs), combined with catalysts and heat to induce polymerization. The process is complex, but in the end leads to the formation of furfuryl alcohol resins in wood cavities and cell walls, leading to major strength improvements @mubarok_2022_modification. Other methods have been developed as well for chemical impregnation @gao_2025_epoxyaminated @bingbinkuai_2022_development, but these methods involve modification of lignin within the wood as well, which will be discussed later and are key to the method used to obtain the results obtained in this paper.

The main drawback of chemical impregnation is the effect it has on the overall sustainability and environmental-friendliness of the treated wood. Chemical treatments must be highly controlled and kept under strict safety regulations, in comparison to the relative ease of compression (which requires only a heated press and basic workspace safety regulations to prevent injury). The chemicals are often hard and/or expensive to obtain, dramatically increasing the price of the material and thus not scaling well to bulk-modification processes when compared with mechanical treatments. The chemicals used may also contribute to reduced sustainability and more environmental pollution @cabral_2022_densification. For these reasons, chemical impregnation was not used in this paper.

== Overview of a Chemical-Mechanical Hybrid Treatment
As both mechanical and chemical methods to densification face problems in their own domains and end up sacrificing important qualities of wood to obtain their results, hybrid methods are necessary to take full advantage of their respective strengths. Thermo-mechanical compression presents the safer, cheaper, and easier methodology, and would be preferred if not for the springback effect, which is only partially mitigated by the heating process. However, the effect is caused by lignin, and thus a pre-treatment to lower lignin concentrations could have substantial effect in lowering springback and internal stresses, while also increasing the amount of potential compression that can be done @song_2018_processing.

The landmark demonstration of this hybrid approach was reported by Song _et al._ (2018), who developed a two-step process to transform natural wood into a high-performance structural material in bulk with improvements in strength and toughness exceeding an order of magnitude. The treatment begins with the partial removal of lignin and hemicellulose from natural wood through boiling in an alkaline solution of sodium hydroxide and sodium sulfite, partially delignifying the wood while reducing the rigidity of the wood's cell wall matrix, thus making it far more compressible. This is followed up with hot-pressing at elevated temperatures and pressures to collapse lumina and porous cell walls throughout the wood sample. What results is a sample of a fifth of the thickness and triple the density, highly packed and aligned cell walls and cellulose nanofibers.

This hybrid treatment led to remarkably improved mechanical properties, with more than tenfold increases in tensile strength, toughness, and ballistic resistance. Normalizing against density, the specific strength of this compressed wood exceeded many structural metals and alloys. The densified wood also showed improved hardness and stability against humidity.

This hybrid treatment, using partial delignification to enhance existing thermo-mechanical densification processes, allows for a more efficient and cost-effective approach than could be attained by chemical or mechanical methods individually, addressing the limitations of both while still reaching the aims set out by this project @song_2018_processing.

