== The Nature of Wood
As mentioned before, the mechanical strength of wood is highly correlated to its density @niklas_2010_worldwide. Thus, altering the internal structure and chemistry of wood is a potential method to unlocking better mechanical performance from wood. One part of this was mentioned as breaking the primary bonds found between cellulose fibers and lignin macromolecules, allowing for greater compression with less internal resistance. Wood's mechanical properties arise from its hierarchical structure of cell walls creating aligned fibers bundled together by the long-chain polymers of hemicellulose and lignin. This structure makes the properties of wood highly directional and sensitive to moisture and loading mode. Thus, treatments can work by selectively softening, removing, or rearranging structural components to make wood more stable dimensionally under deep compression. All of wood's properties can be tied directly back to its biology, as is demonstrated in @tree.

