=== Directional Mechanics
Wood, by nature, is a highly anisotropic material. This leads to very different properties along the grain and across the grain by large factors, especially with regards to stiffness and strength. When wood is used for construction purposes, it is usually oriented so that the main load-bearing elements are oriented along the stem axis parallel to the grain, where it is very strong and stiff. Wood is much weaker and prone to crushing or splitting perpendicular to the grain, so it is not used in that direction for bearing loads. Columns and beams rely on this strong parallel-to-grain behavior that allows wood to bear great loads that test its compressive and flexural strength. On the other hand, the perpendicular-to-grain load at supports, connections, and bearings form an obvious weak point, and in fact are often where structures constructed of wood often fail.

Wood is mainly composed of three chemical compounds: cellulose, hemicellulose, and lignin. Cellulose is the main structural component and forms what are known as the “grains” of the wood. The grain direction, then, is the direction along which the cellulose fibers run. Wood is anisotropic, or has differing material strength depending on direction, due to the directionality of cellulose fibers. Cellulose fibers have high tensile strength in the direction they run, causing the tensile strength along the grain to be far greater than that perpendicular to the grain.

When examining the properties of wood perpendicular to the grain, the other two main components of wood become relevant, as they are used in connecting cellulose fibers perpendicular to the grain. Hemicellulose acts as a more local connector, combining with pectin to cross-link cellulose fibers and increase local strength perpendicular to the grain. Thus hemicellulose, with its high similarity to cellulose, bundles individual cellulose fibers into a larger matrix, enhancing toughness while allowing for more flexibility. Hemicellulose also controls the spacing of microfibrils, acting as a spacer to prevent aggregation of cellulose fibers and keep the local biological structure intact @zhang_2022_chemical.

=== The Role of Lignin
While hemicellulose is similar to cellulose, just being more branched and having shorter chains, lignin bears little similarity to the other two. Cellulose and hemicellulose mostly run along one direction, but lignin is a complex, three-dimensional polymer that serves as the glue holding the wood together. Lignin is a cross-linked polyphenol, consisting of three main types of monolignol monomers: p-coumaryl, coniferyl, and sinapyl alcohols @azadi_2013_liquid. These monomers polymerize to create the “units” that make up lignin, these being p-hydroxyphenyl (H), guaiacyl (G), and syringyl (S) units. The monolignols are derivatives of phenylalanine and differ in content and degree of methoxylation. Softwoods contain more coniferyl alcohol, while hardwoods contain equal amounts of coniferyl and sinapyl alcohol. The types of bonds formed by polymerization are diverse, and thus lignin molecules can be very different depending on the monomer content @georgs_2025_a.

Lignin acts as a binder for the wood over longer distances than hemicellulose, cross-linking hemicellulose chains and filling the space between cellulose-hemicellulose networks to provide a continuous 3D matrix across the cell wall. Due to its irregular shape that holds together far-reaching parts of wood, lignin is the main component resisting compressive loads, which cellulose is weak to. Lignin is more hydrophobic than the other two compounds, which are polysaccharides, and does not crystallize or align like cellulose. Thus, lignin adds stiffness, hardness, water-resistance, and decay-resistance to wood, helping it keep the overall biological structure needed to support enormous structures like trees @balk_2023_lignin.

While hemicellulose and lignin do increase the strength of wood, they exist more so to ensure the biological structure of wood and use their mechanical strength in order to bring this about. However, even with lignin and hemicellulose to fill some of the open areas between cellulose fibers, 60-70% of the wood may be composed of void space, containing air, depending on wood species. This air leads to wood being lightweight with a high strength-to-weight ratio. As mentioned before, collapsing this void space can cause dramatic increases in the density and strength of wood @song_2018_processing. However, the structural compounds of wood, especially lignin, resist compression and limit the effectiveness of a purely compression-based procedure. Thus, it is beneficial to lower the concentration of lignin within a wood sample to allow for better compression of said sample.

=== Partial Delignification
Many procedures have been created for the process of delignification, which is used widely in the paper and pulp industry. Biomass delignification is done through a process of lignin functionalization, followed by dissolution. Functionalization is the process of sufficiently altering the structure and properties of lignin so that it can be dissolved in solution and removed, preferably without large-scale removal of cellulose as well. Among methods used, the most common are sulfite pulping, Kraft pulping, and organosolv methods @schieppati_2023_chemical.

The problem with these methods is that they are tuned for maximum delignification. However, lignin and hemicellulose are essential parts of the structure of wood, and thus maximally removing these compounds would leave a pulp with little material strength. Thus, the method used in this paper is derived from the method determined by Song et al. (2018), which is optimized specifically for partial delignification.

The method used in that paper is most accurately described as alkaline sulfite-assisted partial delignification. The main actor is sodium hydroxide, which swells cell walls within the wood and cleaves the β-O-4 ether linkages in lignin. By only targeting one type of bond within lignin, this method has a maximum lignin dissolution, which is desirable when only aiming for partial lignin dissolution. The sodium hydroxide then solubilizes the lignin fragments left over by the cleavage. Sodium hydroxide, being an extremely powerful base, also attacks the hemicellulose and cellulose within the wood. Cellulose, by the nature of its very linear polymerization and strong C-C bonds, faces much less dissolution than lignin and the more branched polymer of hemicellulose @schieppati_2023_chemical.

Due to the nature of lignin, this method alone would not be enough to ensure delignification, as lignin fragments would likely condense and repolymerize, effectively rendering the entire process useless. Therefore, this delignification process is “sulfite-assisted,” meaning that sodium sulfite acts as a lignin fragment stabilizer that improves lignin solubility, suppresses repolymerization, making the delignification process more controlled and uniform. In the process done in Song et al. (2018), they were able to use this process with varying times and concentrations to remove variable amounts of lignin without large loss of cellulose (up to 99.38% lignin removal with only 29.11% of cellulose lost).

== Achieving Thermo-Mechanical Compression
Once the wood has been pre-treated with partial delignification, it is ready for thermo-mechanical compression. As mentioned before, this process is used to remove void spaces within the wood, immensely increasing density and therefore mechanical strength @niklas_2010_worldwide, while the thermal part of thermo-mechanical compression is meant to prevent springback. However, as an additional benefit, heat-treatment of wood causes the cell lumens to “seal,” limiting the flow of free water within the cell and decreasing hygroscopicity. This helps to improve the resistance of the compressed wood to rotting.

Wood can be compressed along the tangential, longitudinal, and radial directions. The direction that is commonly referred to as “along the grain” is known as the longitudinal direction in wood science. The radial direction refers to that starting at the center of a tree and extending outwards toward the bark, while the tangential direction is that encircling the bark. Due to the nature of wood, compression along the tangential direction would cause the wood to buckle and would be pressed into a wave shape. Longitudinal compression would fare even worse, and the low compressive strength of cellulose would become apparent through the produced folds and microscopic damage @luan_2021_wood. Thus, for most effective compression, wood samples should be compressed along the radial direction, as was done in this paper.

Thus, the method used for this project to densify the wood samples was thermo-mechanical compression in the radial direction. Since the wood has already been partially delignified and hot-pressing is being used to minimize springback, the wood will undergo plastic deformation instead of elastic compression, as is intended so as to permanently restructure the wood samples internally. The heating softens lignin and hemicellulose, while the pressure collapses the lumens and brings the cellulose microfibrils into closer contact. Once the wood cools after compression, new hydrogen bonds form in a denser configuration, while the removal of water causes the structure to lock in place. This produces higher-density and higher-strength wood, while being more water and rot-resistant. The process explained here is visualized in @densifying.

